Why Each Component Was Added

1. Additional Server
A new server was added to split the roles of the infrastructure:
- Web server runs separately.
- Application server runs independently.
- Database sits on its own server.
Why this was added:
- Improves reliability by isolating failures. 
- Allows each component to scale independently.
- Reduces resource contention (CPU, RAM, disk).
- Makes the system more modular and maintainable.

2. Load Balancer Cluster (Two HAProxy Instances)
Another HAProxy load balancer was added and configured to form a cluster with the existing load balancer.
Why added:
- Removes the load balancer as a Single Point of Failure.
- If LB1 fails, LB2 takes over and continues serving traffic.
- Provides high availability and uninterrupted service.

Clustering ensures:
- Shared virtual IP (VIP)
- Automatic failover through keepalived/VRRP
- Continuous service even during maintenance or failures

3. Split Components into Dedicated Servers
The infrastructure was redesigned so each component runs on its own server:
- One server dedicated to the Nginx web server.
- One server dedicated to the application server.
- One server dedicated to the MySQL database.

Why added:
- Scaling becomes easier: you can add more web servers without changing the app or DB layer.
- Better performance: each component uses dedicated resources.
- More security: the database is isolated from the public-facing services.
- Easier debugging: issues can be traced to a specific component layer.


Additional Notes

Application Server vs Web Server
- Web Server (Nginx):
  - Handles and serves static files (HTML, CSS, JS, images).
  - Processes incoming HTTP/HTTPS requests.
  - Passes dynamic requests to the application server.

- Application Server:
  - Runs backend logic.
  - Executes the application's  code.
  - Handles dynamic content and interacts with the database.
  - Returns processed data to the web server.

Splitting these roles into separate servers improves performance and makes scaling straightforward.
